## April Recall Rundown

When it comes to food safety, April 2026 did not disappoint in maintaining a colorful yet concerning roster of recalls. Among the 84 recalls logged for the month, notable trends emerged, ranging from undeclared allergens in favorites like Kirkland Signature Madeleines to rather unusual issues such as hard corn starch lumps in CHIPS AHOY! products.

### The Allergen Alarm
Of the various recalls, 209 so far this year were categorized as Class II, indicating a potential but not immediate health risk. This year alone has seen an uncomfortable number of recalls due to allergens, with undeclared substances like peanuts, milk, and soy becoming recurring culprits.

A particularly concerning case is the Kirkland Signature Traditional Madeleines recall, where undeclared hazelnut and soy lecithin posed a serious risk to consumers with allergies. The oversight in labeling these allergens on a product reaching a wide consumer base, such as Costco, underscores the pressing need for stringent measures preventing such lapses.

### A Crunchy Predicament
CHIPS AHOY! led headlines for more than just nostalgia-fueled advertising when their "Brookie" baked bites were found to contain hard corn starch lumps. While the issue was classified as a Class II recall, this kind of physical contamination could pose a choking hazard and is unacceptable for a brand with such a significant market presence.

### Top Offenders
The year-to-date statistics highlight repeat offenders like Moonlight Packing Corporation and CHETAK NEW YORK LLC, whose track records of recalls are unsettling. While their offenses range in severity, the frequency raises questions about systemic issues and oversight in their production processes.

### A Call to Accountability
With repeat offenders making up 67 of this year's recalls, it is evident that something needs to change. Whether it involves more stringent inspections, stricter penalties for repeat violations, or better consumer education, action is needed to restore trust and safety in the market.

## Conclusion
As we look forward, the shadow of these recalls looms large. Consumers must remain informed and proactive about the food they consume, but the responsibility lies primarily with manufacturers and regulators to ensure safety and transparency. Let's hope for fewer, better-managed recalls in the months to come.
